boe","title":"approximateMinimumDominatingSet(weight:)","kind":"symbol"},"doc://Covering/documentation/Covering/GraphProtocols/Graph/lexicographicallyFirstMaximumIndependentSet()":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The lexicographically first maximum independent set: of all the largest ones, the least by"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vertex index, in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" order; [] for the empty graph. Of two sets of one size, the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"lesser holds the least vertex of their symmetric difference (the rule of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"lexicographicallyFirstMaximumClique()"},{"type":"text","text":", so on a loop-free graph this is that clique of the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"complement). A vertex with a self-loop is never in it; parallel edges count once."}],"url":"\/documentation\/covering\/graphprotocols\/graph\/lexicographicallyfirstmaximumindependentset()","title":"lexicographicallyFirstMaximumIndependentSet()","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/Covering\/documentation\/Covering\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/lexicographicallyFirstMaximumIndependentSet()","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"lexicographicallyFirstMaximumIndependentSet"},{"kind":"text","text":"() -> ["},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Self"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P6VertexQa","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Vertex"},{"kind":"text","text":"]"}],"kind":"symbol"},"doc://Covering/documentation/Covering/GraphProtocols/Graph/maximumIndependentSet()":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Covering\/documentation\/Covering\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/maximumIndependentSet()","fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"maximumIndependentSet","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> [","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P6VertexQa","text":"Vertex","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"]","kind":"text"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A maximum independent set (exact), in "},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" order; [] for the empty graph. A vertex"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"with a self-loop is never in it; parallel edges count once. Deterministic, but which"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"maximum set it is depends on the search; for one fixed by a rule, see"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"lexicographicallyFirstMaximumIndependentSet()","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"topic","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/covering\/graphprotocols\/graph\/maximumindependentset()","title":"maximumIndependentSet()","kind":"symbol"},"doc://Covering/documentation/Covering/GraphProtocols/Graph/approximateMinimumVertexCover(weight:)":{"title":"approximateMinimumVertexCover(weight:)","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A vertex cover of at most twice the least weight (Bar-Yehuda and Even’s local ratio): each"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"edge in position order with neither end in the cover puts in the end with the lesser"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"remaining weight (the end with the lesser vertex index on ties) and subtracts that weight"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"from the other end’s. A self-loop puts its vertex in. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"weight"},{"type":"text","text":" is called once per vertex, in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" order. O(n + m)."}],"url":"\/documentation\/covering\/graphprotocols\/graph\/approximateminimumvertexcover(weight:)","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/Covering\/documentation\/Covering\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/approximateMinimumVer